  "problem": "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y,$ then $D({x}^{2}+1)=$",
  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=5.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{2}+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{2}+1) & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+1)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =7({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(7-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
